# Bounded binary HTTP evidence

Generated by `ddd recipe bounded-binary-http --dry-run`, then the same command
without `--dry-run`. The recipe has no framework imports, runtime dependencies,
module registration, admission decisions or retry policy.

`BoundedBinaryHttpClient` takes a fixed exact origin (scheme, host and port),
explicit request and response header allowlists, optional server headers, and
bounds for time and request/response bytes. HTTPS is required unless trusted
internal HTTP is explicitly configured. Caller dictionaries cannot replace
server headers. Headers, path segments, query parameters and actual bodies are
bounded. Path segments are percent-encoded by the client.
Nonempty segments are required. An explicit boolean `trailingSlash: true`
appends one slash for a fixed collection path such as `/files/`; the default
and `false` leave the encoded path unchanged. This creates no new origin or
redirect authority.

`request('HEAD' | 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PATCH', pathSegments, options)` snapshots a
`Uint8Array` request body before asynchronous transport. It makes exactly one
attempt with redirects rejected, credentials omitted and caching disabled.
The complete operation, including stalled body reads, uses one absolute
monotonic deadline and supports caller cancellation. The cutoff is checked
after transport, headers and body; body collection never renews it. A late
HEAD is denied even when timer callbacks are delayed. Zero-byte streams cannot
starve the cutoff. A late response is cancelled even when an
injected transport ignores abort. A rejected POST/PATCH may have reached its
receiver; consumers must preserve an uncertain outcome rather than retry it.

The response contains status, a frozen selected header dictionary and an owned
byte buffer. HEAD returns no bytes; its Content-Length is bounded numeric
metadata and is not limited by the GET response cap. Content-Length never
substitutes for actual body bounds. Selected Location, ETag, content type,
offsets and other headers are evidence only. Consumers must validate exact
resource/origin bindings before using them. No response header creates
credential, redirect, object or domain authority.

`readBoundedBinaryBody(stream, {maxBytes, timeoutMs?, signal?})` bounds incoming
actual bytes before returning a new snapshot. Do not call an unbounded
`arrayBuffer()` on an untrusted incoming request first. The helper cancels a
stalled, oversized or broken stream and distinguishes body limit, invalid body,
timeout and caller cancellation. The byte limit is at most 16 MiB and the
deadline at most 30 seconds. Callers own any wider product/file policy.

`BoundedBinaryHttpUnavailableError` exposes only a safe reason and optional
numeric HTTP status, never a URL, header, payload or cause. Configuration and
request validation fail before dispatch. Consumer policies own authentication,
current consent, immutable commitments, native completion, idempotency ledgers,
restore fencing and content release. This recipe does not prove any of them.
